class MyQueue {
public:
    stack<int> s1, s2;

    MyQueue() {}

    void push(int x) {
        while (!s1.empty()) {
            s2.push(s1.top());
            s1.pop();
        }

        s1.push(x);

        while (!s2.empty()) {
            s1.push(s2.top());
            s2.pop();
        }
    }

    int pop() {
        int x = s1.top();
        s1.pop();
        return x;
    }

    int peek() {
        return s1.top();
    }

    bool empty() {
        return s1.empty();
    }
};



Intuition: A queue follows FIFO, while a stack follows LIFO. We use two stacks to rearrange elements so the oldest element stays on top.
Algorithm: Move all elements from s1 to s2, insert the new element into s1, then move everything back.
Push: New element is placed at the bottom of s1.
Pop: Remove the top element of s1, which is the oldest element.
Peek: Return the top element of s1 without removing it.
Empty: Check whether s1 is empty.
Time Complexity: O(n) for push, O(1) for pop, O(1) for peek.
Space Complexity: O(n).
